{"content":"Processors subscribe to operations and fold them into query-shaped stores such as search indexes, dashboards, or analytics tables.

Evidence: "Derived read models — processors fold operations into search indexes, dashboards, or analytics without re-querying the source."

This holds when processors can replay or consume the operation stream and tolerate eventual consistency; read models that must be strongly consistent with the document at every instant are a poor fit. It predicts that adding a new query view means adding a processor and replaying history, not changing the document store.","updatedAt":"2026-11-03T19:31:14.046Z"}

{"content":"A processor runs a one-way data flow: operations arrive through `onOperations`, and every write leaves through `dispatch`, the `IProcessorDispatch` handed to the factory on the host module. At e0f277d it has three members, and all three resolve once the write is **accepted**, answering with the job's `{ id, status }` rather than waiting for the commit:

```typescript
interface IProcessorDispatch {
  execute(docId: string, branch: string, actions: Action[],
          signal?: AbortSignal, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<ProcessorDispatchResult>;
  createInDrive(driveId: string, document: PHDocument, parentFolder?: string,
                signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<ProcessorDispatchResult & { documentId: string }>;
  deleteDocument(docId: string, propagate?: "none" | "cascade",
                 signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<ProcessorDispatchResult>;
}
type ProcessorDispatchResult = { id: string; status: string };
```

- `execute` submits actions on one document. In the reactor's host module it calls the client's `executeAsync`, so the returned `status` is the status at submission.
- `createInDrive` adds a document to a drive (under `parentFolder` when given) and also returns the new `documentId`. When it resolves, the document's own creation has completed and the drive's file node has been accepted.
- `deleteDocument` deletes a document; `"cascade"` deletes its orphaned children too.

The canonical shape is a processor that watches for a state transition and fires an action at a different document:

```typescript
if (operation.action.type === "SET_STATUS" &&
    operation.action.input?.status === "approved") {
  await this.dispatch.execute(this.paymentDocId, "main", [
    { type: "CREATE_PAYMENT",
      input: { invoiceId: context.documentId, amount: operation.action.input.amount },
      scope: "global" },
  ]);
}
```

The processor dispatches and moves on. If it needs the result, it widens its own `ProcessorFilter` to include the target so the resulting operations come back through a later `onOperations` call, instead of waiting for them. Waiting is the anti-pattern recorded in "Waiting for a write to reach READ_READY from inside a processor is an anti-pattern".

Delivery to processors is at-least-once (restart, retry, and a backfill racing a live batch can all repeat an operation), so a dispatching processor needs an idempotence guard: check the target document's state, or a dedup key in it, before dispatching, so a repeated batch does not fire the side action twice.

Checked against `packages/shared/processors/types.ts` (`IProcessorDispatch`) and `packages/reactor/src/processors/host-module.ts` (`createReactorHostModuleBase`) at e0f277d.

Provenance: academy `03-Build/04-WorkWithData/05-ProcessorBestPractices.md`, first confirmed against `packages/shared/processors/types.ts` and `apps/connect/src/store/processor-host-module.ts` at 6.2.2-dev.45.

## Revision history
- v6.2.3-dev.91+e0f277d (2026-11-03): `dispatch` is no longer execute-only. `createInDrive` and `deleteDocument` were added and the page now says processors write through `dispatch` and read through `module.read` (65e2121269 "processors read through module.read and write through dispatch"). Title widened from `dispatch.execute()` to `dispatch`. The page no longer describes `execute` as wrapping `reactorClient.executeAsync`, but the reactor host module still implements it that way.","updatedAt":"2026-11-03T19:36:17.596Z"}

{"content":"A processor writes through `dispatch` and reads through `module.read`. From inside `onOperations` it must not wait for its write to reach `READ_READY`, or read the written document back.

The reason is the projection pipeline. The read-model coordinator runs each batch of a projection chain in three steps: pre-ready read models index it, `JOB_READ_READY` is emitted, then the post-ready stage (where the processor manager delivers to processors) runs, and the chain's next batch starts only after that. A processor also receives one `onOperations` call at a time. So the `READ_READY` of a write the processor just made can sit behind an earlier batch whose post-ready stage is waiting for this very processor to return, and the processor is waiting for that `READ_READY`.

The wait has several spellings, and all are the anti-pattern:

```typescript
// Bad: blocks until the job is READ_READY
const result = await this.dispatch.execute(docId, "main", actions);
await module.read.waitForJob(result.id);

// Also bad: the deprecated facade resolves with the updated document, i.e. after READ_READY
await module.client.execute(docId, "main", actions);

// Good: resolves once the write is accepted
await this.dispatch.execute(docId, "main", actions);
```

Three reasons, in order of severity:

1. **Potential blocking.** `READ_READY` waits on processors finishing their current batch, including the caller.
2. **Unnecessary round-trip.** The resulting operations arrive through `onOperations` anyway, so waiting buys latency and nothing else.
3. **One-way flow.** React to operations, dispatch new work, move on. If you need the result, handle it in a later `onOperations` call.

The type surface does not enforce this. At e0f277d the host module still carries `client: IReactorClient`, marked `@deprecated` in favour of `read` and `dispatch`, and `read.waitForJob` is public; both compile from inside a processor. The guardrail is the convention.

Checked against `packages/reactor/src/read-models/coordinator.ts` (`runBatch`), `packages/reactor/src/processors/host-module.ts` and `packages/reactor/src/client/types.ts` (the facade `execute` returns the updated document; `write.execute` returns a `WriteHandle` whose `readable` is the `READ_READY` wait) at e0f277d.

## Revision history
- v6.2.3-dev.91+e0f277d (2026-11-03): widened from "calling reactorClient.execute() blocks until READ_READY" to any wait for `READ_READY` from inside a processor. The page's warning was renamed from "Why not reactorClient?" to "Why not wait for the write?", and its bad example is now `dispatch.execute` followed by `module.read.waitForJob` (65e2121269). `module.client` is deprecated, and the reactor client split into read and write halves whose `write.execute` no longer blocks.","updatedAt":"2026-11-03T19:36:21.213Z"}

{"content":"## The claim
When a document is deleted or purged, the reactor removes its own records of it: operations, snapshots, its indexes. The rows, series or files a processor derived from that document stay until the processor removes them. There is no purge callback and `IProcessor` has no other hook for it, so a processor that ignores deletions keeps a purged document's data indefinitely, and nothing later will catch it up.

## How a processor erases
- **Handle `DELETE_DOCUMENT` and `PURGE_DOCUMENT` alike.** Both name the document in `operation.action.input.documentId`. `PURGE_DOCUMENT` is the marker a purge leaves in the log; a reactor that missed the deletion (a peer that synced late) receives only the marker.
- **Put `"document"` in the filter's `scope`.** Both are `document`-scope operations, so a processor filtered to `["global"]` never sees them. Skip the other `document`-scope operations yourself (codegen's relational template still lets `CREATE_DOCUMENT` through, so a document that was only created gets its row).
- **Handle deletions before parsing `resultingState`.** The marker can arrive with none.
- **Make the handler idempotent.** The marker can arrive after the document's `DELETE_DOCUMENT`, and more than once.
- **Do not erase in `onDisconnect`.** It also runs when the factory is unregistered or reloaded, and reactor-api reloads every package on each package change, so erasing there would wipe live data on a reload.

```typescript
async onOperations(operations: OperationWithContext[]): Promise<void> {
  for (const { operation, context } of operations) {
    const type = operation.action.type;
    if (type === "DELETE_DOCUMENT" || type === "PURGE_DOCUMENT") {
      const input = operation.action.input as { documentId?: string };
      await this.store.deleteDocument(input.documentId ?? context.documentId);
      continue;
    }
    if (context.scope !== "global") continue;
    // ...index the operation
  }
}
```

After a purge the reactor delivers the document's marker and none of its earlier operations, whether routed live or read by a backfill. The check takes no lock, so a delivery racing the purge can still land; the marker that follows erases it.

## Helpers
`RelationalDbProcessor` has `deleteDocumentRows`, `isNamespaceDrive` and `dropNamespace`. An analytics processor that writes a document's series under `ph/doc/<documentId>/...` clears them with `analyticsStore.clearSeriesBySource(AnalyticsPath.fromString(`ph/doc/${documentId}`), true)`. The generated relational and analytics processors include this handling.

## Where it sits
This is the processor side of one boundary in "Reactor erasure stops at the reactor's own storage…" (processors that ignore deletions keep the data). A processor that does erase can still write one late row when its store is on another database handle ("Read models on another database handle are not fenced against a purge…").

## Evidence
Academy › Build › WorkWithData › Processor best practices, § "Erasing deleted documents". The `IProcessor` contract in `packages/shared/processors/types.ts` at e0f277d ("Erase a document's data on either deletion action"; `onDisconnect` "also runs when the factory is unregistered, so it is not a deletion signal") and `packages/codegen/src/templates/processors/relational-db/processor.ts`.","updatedAt":"2026-11-03T19:37:45.817Z"}

