- v6.2.3-dev.91+e0f277d (2026-11-03): the target argument is now `documentIdOrSlug: String` (id or slug) instead of `docId: PHID!`, which remains as a deprecated alias; `document(identifier:)` became `document(idOrSlug:)` and `parentIdentifier` became `parentIdOrSlug`. Schema change 2842b3ef1e "name per-model subgraph document arguments by what they accept" (first in v6.2.3-dev.35); docs 1485fa80bb "show the new document argument names". The templates now live in `model-schema-templates.ts`.","updatedAt":"2026-11-03T19:42:41.845Z"}

{"content":"## Walking a drive from a resolver

A custom subgraph that searches or aggregates across a drive needs the drive's contents. In v6 that is a relationship query on the reactor's read client, not a walk over a `nodes` array. At e0f277d the resolver takes the read half from its context:

```ts
import type { BaseSubgraph, Context } from "@powerhousedao/reactor-api";

export const getResolvers = (subgraph: BaseSubgraph) => {
  return {
    Query: {
      searchTodos: async (
        parent: unknown,
        args: { driveId: string; searchTerm: string },
        ctx: Context,
      ) => {
        const children = await ctx.clients.read.getOutgoingRelationships(
          args.driveId,
          "child",
        );
        const todoItems: string[] = [];
        for (const doc of children.results) {
          if (doc.header.documentType !== "powerhouse/todo-list") continue;
          const todoDoc = doc as TodoListDocument;
          const hits = todoDoc.state.global.items.filter((e) =>
            e.text.includes(args.searchTerm),
          ).length;
          if (hits > 0) todoItems.push(doc.header.id);
        }
        return todoItems;
      },
    },
  };
};
```

Three details to carry over into your own resolvers:

- **`"child"` is the relationship type.** Drive membership is modelled as an outgoing relationship from the drive document, which is why the same call generalises to any relationship you define.
- **Results come back as a page**, read via `children.results`, not a bare array. Large drives require paging, so a resolver that assumes one page is correct only by accident.
- **Type filtering is yours to do.** The call returns documents of every type in the drive; each carries `header.documentType`, and skipping the ones you do not care about is resolver code, not a query parameter.

The built-in reactor resolvers make the same `read.getOutgoingRelationships` call (`packages/reactor-api/src/graphql/reactor/resolvers.ts` at e0f277d), so this is the supported path rather than a workaround. The resolver reads through `ctx.clients.read` because that is the per-request read half; `subgraph.reactorClient` still compiles but is a deprecated facade, and outside a resolver (schema setup) the subgraph's `readClient` is the equivalent.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/04-WorkWithData/03-UsingSubgraphs.md; first written against packages/reactor-api/src/graphql/reactor/resolvers.ts at v6.2.2-dev.45._

