{"content":"Processors run in parallel, each on its own delivery queue. When `onOperations` throws, or a backfill page cannot be read, the manager:

1. moves the processor to `status: "errored"`;
2. records `lastError` and `lastErrorTimestamp` on its `TrackedProcessor`;
3. sets its cursor **below the lowest ordinal of the batch that failed**. When queued live batches were merged into one call, that is the lowest ordinal of the merged call.

Other processors are unaffected: the failure is contained to one processor on one drive, not the batch and not the reactor. Later batches that match the errored processor are skipped and pull its cursor below them likewise, so the cursor marks the oldest operation it has not taken and nothing is lost. Once revived, it replays from there.

**There is no automatic retry.** An errored processor stays errored and is skipped on every later batch until one of:

- `tracked.retry()` — re-activates the processor and replays from its cursor.
- Re-registering its factory. Registering the same `identifier` unregisters the prior factory first. Unregistering *releases* the factory's cursor rows rather than keeping them as positions, so the re-registered processor starts afresh per its `startFrom`, not from the held cursor: with `"beginning"` it replays the drive's whole history, with `"current"` the operations it failed on are never redelivered. (This consequence is an inference from the unregister rule in the reference and commit 34e14393cb.) Prefer `retry()` to recover an errored processor.

One failure does **not** error the processor: a check for purged documents (the tombstone lookup) that keeps failing, as during a database failover. After about 80 seconds of backoff the processor parks instead, stays `active`, and retries every 30 seconds (see "A processor parks through a tombstone-lookup outage…"). An errored processor still receives its drive's deletion.

Nothing about an errored processor is visible unless you look. Inspect via the manager: `get(processorId)` for one, `getAll()` for every tracked processor across all drives.

```typescript
type TrackedProcessor = {
  processorId: string;       // `${factoryId}:${driveId}:${slot}`; slot is the record's index by default
  factoryId: string;
  driveId: string;
  processorIndex: number;
  record: ProcessorRecord;
  lastOrdinal: number;
  status: ProcessorStatus;   // "active" | "errored"
  lastError: string | undefined;
  lastErrorTimestamp: Date | undefined;
  retry: () => Promise<void>;
};
```

So a production processor needs a health check that walks `getAll()` for `status: "errored"`, and for an `active` processor with a `lastError` (parked); otherwise a read model quietly stops updating and the only symptom is stale data. `onDisconnect` is treated differently: a throw there is caught and logged, never propagated, and does not error the processor.

Checked against `packages/reactor/src/processors/processor-queue.ts` and `processor-manager.ts` at e0f277d.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/05-Processors.md (first written against Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._

## Revision history
- v6.2.3-dev.91+e0f277d (2026-11-03): "its cursor freezes" became "its cursor is held below the failed batch", and later skipped batches pull it lower. Failed merged calls park below their lowest ordinal (002dc6821b "deliver queued live batches to a processor in one call"; d968ebef99). `retry()` replays from the cursor. A tombstone-lookup outage parks rather than errors (2e57eb5faf). Re-registration releases cursor rows (34e14393cb), so it restarts per `startFrom` instead of resuming.","updatedAt":"2026-11-03T19:48:58.187Z"}

{"content":"`startFrom` is an optional field on a `ProcessorRecord`:

```typescript
type ProcessorRecord = {
  processor: IProcessor;
  filter: ProcessorFilter;
  startFrom?: "beginning" | "current";
  replayState?: "latest" | "collapse" | "exact" | "operations";
  id?: string;
};
```

Its semantics are narrower than the name suggests. It is consulted **only when no cursor row positions the processor**:

- `"beginning"` (the default) starts the cursor at ordinal 0, so the processor backfills the drive's full history.
- `"current"` starts the cursor just below the drive's creation when the processor is created from the drive's creation batch, and at the manager's current ordinal when a late `registerFactory` creates it. So it sees either its drive's whole history (a drive born after registration) or only what comes next (a drive that already existed).

Once a positioning cursor row exists, `startFrom` is **ignored** — the persisted cursor wins, and restarting the reactor never re-runs it. Flipping a deployed processor from `"current"` to `"beginning"` and restarting will not backfill it. Two things do reposition it:

- `tracked.retry()` replays from the processor's cursor (not from `startFrom`).
- Unregistering the factory marks its cursor rows **released**. A released row does not position a processor, so the next registration starts every processor afresh per `startFrom` (it still owes the deletion of any drive deleted in between). reactor-api unregisters and re-registers every package on each package change, so a package reload, unlike a restart, does apply a changed `startFrom`. The rows of a factory that is never registered again stay in `ProcessorCursor`.

Cursors live in the `ProcessorCursor` table keyed by `processorId` (`${factoryId}:${driveId}:${slot}`; the slot is the record's array index by default, and the record's `id`, namespace or class name when the reactor runs with `legacyProcessorIds` off). On startup the manager rehydrates every cursor and catch-up replays the gap.

**The advance rule.** The cursor, `lastOrdinal`, is the highest ordinal the processor has taken, pulled back below any batch it failed to take. On each batch the manager applies the filter and queues the matches, minus any a backfill has already delivered; on success it **raises** `lastOrdinal` to the batch's maximum ordinal, including unmatched operations, when that is higher. "When higher" matters because batches for different documents arrive out of ordinal order. The cursor moves even when nothing matched, so a narrowly filtered processor does not accumulate a backlog.

**Backfill.** When `lastOrdinal` is behind, the manager pages with `operationIndex.getSinceOrdinal(lastOrdinal)`, filters each page, calls `onOperations`, advances and persists the cursor per page, and follows the continuation until exhausted: on registering against a drive with history, after a restart, and on `retry()`. Backfill runs on the processor's own queue, so other documents keep indexing and other processors keep receiving; live batches for that processor wait behind it and skip what it already delivered. `registerFactory` resolves once the factory has run and its processors are bound, **before** their backfills finish; to wait for one, watch `lastOrdinal` through `get()` or `getAll()`.

Checked against `packages/shared/processors/types.ts` (`ProcessorRecord`) and `packages/reactor/src/processors/utils.ts` (`resolveProcessorSlots`) at e0f277d.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/05-Processors.md (first written against Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._

