{"content":"## The claim
Before a batch is delivered, the processor's queue checks which of its document ids are purged (tombstoned), so a purged document's operations are not handed out. When that lookup keeps failing, as during a database failover, the processor does **not** go errored. After its retries — 10 attempts, from 100 ms doubling to a 30 s cap, about 81 s in all — it parks:

- `status` stays `"active"`, with the failure in `lastError` and `lastErrorTimestamp`;
- its cursor holds below the batch it could not check;
- later batches skip it without holding other processors;
- the lookup is retried every 30 seconds, and each failure is logged ("Processor '…' parked: tombstones unread after 10 retries, retrying every 30000 ms").

When the check answers, `lastError` clears and the processor replays from its cursor. A processor parked when the reactor stops starts active and unparked on the next start, and replays from its cursor.

## Why it is built this way
Before 2e57eb5faf an exhausted lookup made the processor errored; the next write persisted that, a restart kept it errored, and a long failover needed a manual `retry()` on every processor. An `onOperations` throw is the processor's own failure and waits for a human; a lookup outage is the infrastructure's, and now heals by itself.

## So what
`status === "active"` is not the same as healthy. A health check should flag an active processor whose `lastError` is set: it is parked, and its read model is falling behind.

## Evidence
Academy › Reference › Reactor › Processors, § "Catch-up and ordering", the paragraph after Failure isolation. Code at e0f277d: `packages/reactor/src/processors/processor-queue.ts` (`LOOKUP_RETRIES = 10`, `LOOKUP_RETRY_MS = 100`, `MAX_LOOKUP_RETRY_MS = 30_000`, `park`). Commit 2e57eb5faf "park a processor through a tombstone lookup outage".","updatedAt":"2026-11-03T19:52:03.939Z"}

{"content":"## The claim
When a drive is deleted, each of its processors receives the batch up to and including the drive's `DELETE_DOCUMENT` (or its `PURGE_DOCUMENT` marker), and then `onDisconnect`. The drive's own deletion bypasses the filter: a processor whose `documentType` names only `powerhouse/todo-list`, or whose `scope` omits `"document"`, still receives it.

## Why
It is the processor's one chance to drop the storage it keeps for that drive: a per-drive namespace, series under the drive's path. `onDisconnect` cannot serve, because it also runs when the factory is unregistered or reloaded; the deletion operation is the signal.

## How it is used
Codegen's relational processor (and the Relational DB tutorial) treats it as a namespace drop:

```typescript
if (type === "DELETE_DOCUMENT" || type === "PURGE_DOCUMENT") {
  const documentId = input.documentId ?? context.documentId;
  if (this.isNamespaceDrive(documentId)) { await this.dropNamespace(); return; }
  await this.deleteDocumentRows(documentId);
  continue;
}
```

So the generated processor drops its namespace even though its filter names only one document type. A namespace that several drives share must not be dropped on one drive's deletion, which is what `isNamespaceDrive` checks.

## Contrast
The documents inside a drive get no such bypass: a processor sees their `DELETE_DOCUMENT` and `PURGE_DOCUMENT` only if its filter matches them, including the `"document"` scope ("A processor must erase a deleted document's data itself…").

## Evidence
Academy › Reference › Reactor › Processors, § "Deletion and erasure" (the drive-deletion bullet). The `IProcessor.onOperations` contract in `packages/shared/processors/types.ts` at e0f277d ("A drive's processors also receive the drive's own DELETE_DOCUMENT or PURGE_DOCUMENT, whatever the filter, as the last delivery before `onDisconnect`").","updatedAt":"2026-11-03T19:52:10.185Z"}

{"content":"## The claim
`deleteDocumentRows(documentId)` is the relational base class's erasure helper, and it works by convention rather than by knowing the schema. It queries `information_schema` for the processor's namespace, takes every **base table** with a column named `document_id` or `documentId`, and deletes the rows where that column equals the deleted id. A table without either column is not touched, so its rows for the deleted document survive.

```ts
// packages/shared/processors/relational/types.ts at e0f277d
export const DOCUMENT_ID_COLUMNS = ["document_id", "documentId"] as const;
// select c.table_name, c.column_name from information_schema.columns c
//   join information_schema.tables t ... where t.table_type = 'BASE TABLE'
//   and c.column_name in (DOCUMENT_ID_COLUMNS)
// then deleteFrom(table).where(column, "=", documentId) for each
```

## Consequences for schema design
- Give every table a processor writes a `document_id` column recording which document each row came from. The Relational DB tutorial's `todo` table gained `document_id varchar(255) not null` for exactly this.
- A table keyed some other way (an aggregate across documents, a join table, a lookup keyed by a business id) needs its own delete in the same `DELETE_DOCUMENT` / `PURGE_DOCUMENT` branch. The docs say so: "Delete its rows yourself in the same branch."
- The match is on the exact column names, so `doc_id` or `source_document` are invisible to it.
- Views are skipped (`BASE TABLE` only), which is correct: they derive from the tables.

## Where it sits
This is the relational implementation of the erasure duty in "A processor must erase a deleted document's data itself…". For the whole drive's deletion the helper is `dropNamespace`, guarded by `isNamespaceDrive`.

## Evidence
Academy › Build › WorkWithData › Relational database processor, § "Erase a deleted document" (the helper table and "A table without a document_id or documentId column is not touched"). Code: `RelationalDbProcessor.deleteDocumentRows` in `packages/shared/processors/relational/types.ts` at e0f277d.","updatedAt":"2026-11-03T19:59:52.797Z"}

{"content":"## The claim
When a drive is deleted, the relational processor's deletion branch asks `isNamespaceDrive(documentId)` before `dropNamespace()`. At e0f277d that check is true only when all of these hold:

1. the processor was constructed with a drive id (the optional fourth constructor argument, `_driveId`);
2. the deleted id **is** that drive id;
3. `getNamespace(driveId)` returns this processor's namespace;
4. `getNamespace` for a different drive id returns a **different** namespace, so the namespace is this drive's alone.

```ts
protected isNamespaceDrive(documentId: string): boolean {
  const driveId = this._driveId;
  if (driveId === undefined || documentId !== driveId) return false;
  const processorClass = this.constructor as typeof RelationalDbProcessor;
  if (processorClass.getNamespace(driveId) !== this._namespace) return false;
  return processorClass.getNamespace(`${driveId}-other`) !== this._namespace;
}
```

## Consequences
- **No drive id, no drop.** A processor built without the fourth argument never drops its namespace; a deleted drive's tables stay until something else removes them. Codegen's factory passes `driveHeader.id`; hand-written factories must too.
- **Shared namespaces are safe.** A `getNamespace` override that returns one namespace for every drive shares it across drives, so `isNamespaceDrive` is false: the drive's deletion removes only the drive document's own rows, and each of its documents' rows go with that document's own deletion.

## Why it changed
Before 78175ef110 the check compared `getNamespace(documentId)` with the processor's namespace. With a constant override that is true for *any* document, so any document's deletion dropped the shared namespace and every drive's rows with it. Matching by the drive id fixed it.

## Evidence
Academy › Build › WorkWithData › Relational database processor, § "Erase a deleted document" (the `isNamespaceDrive` paragraph) and the factory sample's comment ("The drive id is what `isNamespaceDrive` compares a deleted document's id against"). Code: `packages/shared/processors/relational/types.ts` at e0f277d. Commit 78175ef110 "match a relational processor's own drive by id, not namespace".","updatedAt":"2026-11-03T19:59:59.515Z"}

{"content":"`IDocumentIndexer` (`src/storage/kysely/document-indexer.ts`) is the read model behind every relationship question. It indexes three operations — `ADD_RELATIONSHIP` creates an edge, `UPDATE_RELATIONSHIP` changes its metadata, `REMOVE_RELATIONSHIP` removes it — and its traversal queries are four:

- **`getOutgoing()`** — children and other targets of a document;
- **`getIncoming()`** — parents and other sources;
- **`findPath()`** — path finding by breadth-first search;
- **`findAncestors()`** — the full ancestor graph.

The last two are the ones worth remembering, because they are not derivable from a document's own state at any reasonable cost: a path or an ancestor set spans documents, and each document knows nothing about its parents. The relationship table is indexed on both `sourceId` and `targetId`, which is what makes the graph genuinely bidirectional rather than a parent pointer plus a scan.

`getOutgoing` / `getIncoming` are paged (`{ cursor, limit }`), so enumeration of a large container is DB-native rather than a state read. Beside the four, the interface answers point questions — `hasRelationship`, `getDirectedRelationships`, `getUndirectedRelationships`, `getOrphanedChildren`, `getRelationshipTypes` — and offers `waitForConsistency` for reads that carry a consistency token.

## Provenance
- `packages/reactor/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` — Components › DocumentIndexer

_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._

