{"content":"# operation errors are recorded on operations not thrown to callers

Powerhouse handles reducer errors differently from typical throw/catch patterns. When a reducer throws an error:

1. The operation is **still recorded** in `document.operations.global` (or `.local`)
2. The error message is stored in `operation.error` as a string — and, when the thrown value is a generated error class, its name and code as `operation.errorName` and `operation.errorCode`
3. `reducer(...)` itself returns normally; nothing propagates to the caller
4. The state is **not** rolled back: whatever the reducer changed before the `throw` stays (on base-reducer 1 and 2 documents, the default) — see "On base-reducer 1 and 2 documents a reducer that throws after mutating keeps those mutations". A reducer that validates and throws before mutating leaves the state as it was.

This means error testing looks different:

```typescript
// ✅ Correct — check the operation's error property
const updated = reducer(document, setName({ name: "invalid" }));
expect(updated.operations.global[0].error).toBe("Name is not allowed");
// holds because this reducer throws before changing anything
expect(updated.state.global.name).toBe(initialState);

// ❌ Wrong — errors are not propagated to the caller
expect(() => reducer(document, setName({ name: "invalid" }))).toThrow();
```

Since [[operation error index must match the number of prior dispatches]], the index into `operations.global` depends on how many operations were dispatched before this one in that scope.

The same record is what an editor sees in Connect, through a different door: `dispatch`'s `onErrors` callback receives an `OperationRejectedError` per rejected action, not the document's operation list.

Mechanism: `packages/shared/document-model/reducer.ts` at e0f277d runs the model's reducer inside Mutative's `create()` with a try/catch inside the recipe; the catch writes `error`, `errorName` and `errorCode` on the appended operation and resets the draft state only on the undo-skip path.

---

Topics:
- [[reducer patterns]]

## Revision history
- v6.2.3-dev.91+e0f277d (2026-11-03): point 3 said "the state is NOT mutated"; corrected to "not rolled back — changes made before the throw stay", which the reducers and testing references shipped in 972f193395 state and `packages/shared/document-model/reducer.ts` does for base-reducer 1 and 2 documents. Added errorName/errorCode and the Connect onErrors path.
","updatedAt":"2026-11-03T20:49:30.000Z"}

{"content":"# operation error index must match the number of prior dispatches

When testing reducer errors, the operation index matters:

```typescript
// First operation dispatched → index [0]
const doc1 = reducer(document, addItem({ id: "1", name: "test" }));

// Second operation dispatched → index [1]
const doc2 = reducer(doc1, addItem({ id: "1", name: "duplicate" }));
expect(doc2.operations.global[1].error).toBe("Duplicate ID");

// ❌ Wrong — index [0] is the first (successful) operation
expect(doc2.operations.global[0].error).toBe("Duplicate ID");
```

The index corresponds to the total number of operations dispatched before it **in the same scope**, not the number of errors. Since [[operation errors are recorded on operations not thrown to callers]], every dispatch (successful or failed) produces an operation at the next index of its scope. Global and local scopes keep separate logs, so a local operation dispatched in between does not move the global index.

---

