- `apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/01-WorkingWithTheReactor.md` — 'In a generated processor (server-side)' — the cast removed from the scaffold

_Recorded from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74** during the v6.2.2-dev.74 source sync._","updatedAt":"2026-09-02T22:21:17.897Z"}

{"content":"`subscribeToSource(source, callback)` is the change-notification half of `IAnalyticsStore`. It returns an unsubscribe function rather than taking a handle, so cleanup is a closure:

```typescript
const unsub = store.subscribeToSource(
  AnalyticsPath.fromString("atlas/"),
  (source) => { /* decide whether to requery */ },
);
// later
unsub();
```

Matching follows the same path semantics as the rest of the engine. Given a subscription to `atlas/`:

- `atlas/foo` fires it
- `atlas/foo/bar` fires it
- `rwa/foo` does not

Wildcards work at interior segments too. `atlas/*/test` fires for `atlas/foo/test`, `atlas/another-thing/test`, and `atlas/foo/test/a/b/c` — but not `rwa/foo/test`.

The mechanics in `AnalyticsSubscriptionManager` are worth knowing because they explain a surprise: notification walks the *prefixes of the updated path* and matches them against registered subscriptions. So an update to `/a/b/c` notifies subscribers of `/a/b/c`, `/a/b`, and `/a`. Wildcard patterns are then checked separately, segment by segment, and a wildcard subscription longer than the update path cannot match. Callbacks are wrapped in try/catch and every subscriber is delivered to even if an earlier one throws; the collected errors are re-raised together as a `NotificationError` after delivery completes.

The callback receives the updated `AnalyticsPath`, not the data. It is a hint to requery, which is exactly how `useAnalyticsQuery`'s `sources` option turns a static query into a live one.

Provenance: draft `03-Build/04-WorkWithData/_06-Analytics Engine/typescript/index.md`; behaviour verified in `packages/analytics-engine/core/src/AnalyticsSubscriptionManager.ts` and the interface in `packages/shared/analytics/types.ts` at 6.2.2-dev.45.","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T12:35:55.000Z"}

{"content":"A builder listing that joins profiles, scopes of work, and workstreams can become expensive to assemble on every query. A dedicated read model consolidates those sources for the listing instead of repeatedly composing generic queries.

The source says a read model is “necessary when combining data from multiple (more than two or three) documents, especially at scale, as generic queries and custom subgraphs become inefficient.” It also cautions against “premature optimization,” so this is most useful when cross-document joins and expected scale make query-time composition costly, not automatically for every list.

When those conditions hold, expect a read-optimized model to reduce repeated query work; when the source set is small or query volume is low, generic queries may remain sufficient.","updatedAt":"2026-09-29T16:36:57.059Z"}

